  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally quiet conditions this weekend ahead of the \nnext cold front. \n\nA cold front moved through the area this morning and is now well \nsouth of the area and is draped across the Carolinas. A brief surge \nof drier, cooler air moved in the wake of the front as high pressure \nbuilt in, but winds have already shifted to the east, so it will \nonly be short-lived. Temperatures are currently in the upper 70s to \nlower 80s and dew points are in mid 60s, making for a comfortable \nmid-September afternoon. Satellite imagery has been depicting mostly \ncloudy skies for much of the morning, but cloud cover has finally \nstarted to scatter out some this afternoon. \n\nThe high will quickly move offshore tomorrow as the next front\nmoves into the Ohio River Valley, and winds will shift to the \nsoutheast to south. This will invite the warmer, slightly more \nhumid conditions to the area, with highs creeping back up into \nthe mid to upper 80s (lower 80s for the Eastern Shore). Expect \nto see more breaks of sunshine, especially across southern \ncounties. The front will nudge closer to our northern/western \nCWA border by late Sunday potentially bringing some showers \nalong with it. Sunday night low temperatures will be a smidge \nmilder in the mid to upper 60s as cloud cover and scattered rain\nchances return. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Persistent onshore flow and a potential \nstrengthening coastal low will create a wetter, cooler, and breezier \nforecast next week.\n\nA big shift in the weather pattern is on tap for next week as \nanother front approaches the area, bringing unsettled weather to the \nlocal area. The cold front will slowly drop through the area late \nMonday through Tuesday as an expansive ridge sets up from the Great \nLakes through the Hudson Bay area. The slow progression of the front \nwill allow for rain/storm chances to linger through the day on \nMonday. WPC currently has a Marginal risk for excessive rainfall as \nsome showers/storms could produce areas of localized flooding. SPC \nhas included a Marginal risk (level 1/5) for the far SW piedmont \ncounties, as this is where the warmer temperatures and slightly \nbetter environment will be. Due to the slow movement of the front, \nthere will likely be a sharp temperature gradient across the \nforecast area, with upper 70s possible across the northern half of \nthe area and 85F to near 90F across the southern half of the area.\nGlobal models have an area of low pressure developing along the \nfrontal boundary as it moves through the area. The ridge will shift \na bit eastward on Tuesday, which will move this low offshore of the \nMid-Atlantic region. Guidance has the low strengthening as it lifts \nnortheast, however, there are discrepancies between models in \nthe exact details. Ensemble guidance has a wide range of \npossible strength and various tracks, so there is uncertainty \nregarding exact impacts to our area. This low is forecast to \nslowly lift northeastward, and the gradient between this low and\nthe aforementioned high to the north will tighten, bringing \nincreasing NE winds to the area starting Monday night. The \nduration of the elevated flow, the proximity of the low to the \ncoast, and the strength of the low will all play an important \nrole in how several coastal hazards pan out, so stay tuned to \nthe forecast for next week as this system develops. \n\nWhile the coast is potentially seeing impacts from the low, the \nbroad high will build down to the west of the low and put most of \nthe area in a CAD set-up. This will keep temperatures likely well \nbelow normal through most of the week, especially in the piedmont. \nWith the forecast NE flow, coastal areas will still be modified by \nthe warmer water temperatures, but are still forecast to be below \nnormal mid-late week.",
